/* Verse Games: THE WHOLE COUNSEL · Jeremiah chapters 31-35
   Verse text is filled/verified from the KJV by tools/fill-verses.js */
VG.registerBibleChapters("jeremiah", [
{n:31, name:"The New Covenant", seal:{ref:"Jeremiah 31:33",verse:"But this shall be the covenant that I will make with the house of Israel; After those days, saith the LORD, I will put my law in their inward parts, and write it in their hearts; and will be their God, and they shall be my people."}, facts:["Did you know Jeremiah 31 is the great new covenant chapter? Seven centuries before Christ, God promised to write His law on human hearts, not stone tablets, and Christians read its fulfillment in the cross (Jeremiah 31:31-34).","Did you know Matthew quotes Rachel weeping in Ramah when Herod slaughters the infants of Bethlehem, showing this chapter's grief also foreshadows Christ's own story (Jeremiah 31:15; Matthew 2:16-18)?","Did you know 'I have loved thee with an everlasting love' (Jeremiah 31:3) is one of the most quoted verses in all of Scripture, often called the golden text of God's affection for His people?"], questions:[
{q:"What did the LORD say about His love for Israel?", options:["Yea, I have loved thee with an everlasting love","I have loved thee for thy righteousness","My love lasts while thou art faithful","I have loved thee as the nations do"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 31:3",verse:"The LORD hath appeared of old unto me, saying, Yea, I have loved thee with an everlasting love: therefore with lovingkindness have I drawn thee.", insight:"Everlasting love is not a mood of God but His nature; His affection for His people has no starting point and no ending."},
{q:"What did God promise the virgin of Israel?", options:["Again I will build thee, and thou shalt be built","Thou shalt be forgotten forever","Thou shalt build thyself without help","Thou shalt be built by the nations"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 31:4",verse:"Again I will build thee, and thou shalt be built, O virgin of Israel: thou shalt again be adorned with thy tabrets, and shalt go forth in the dances of them that make merry.", insight:"God Himself is the builder; when He rebuilds, the restoration is adorned with dancing, not bare survival."},
{q:"What would the watchmen upon mount Ephraim cry?", options:["Arise ye, and let us go up to Zion unto the LORD our God","Arise, and flee to the mountains","Arise, and defend your walls","Arise, and mourn for the fallen"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 31:6",verse:"For there shall be a day, that the watchmen upon the mount Ephraim shall cry, Arise ye, and let us go up to Zion unto the LORD our God.", insight:"Watchmen who once cried alarms would cry worship; true security sends God's people toward Him, not away."},
{q:"From where would God bring the returning captives?", options:["From the north country, and from the coasts of the earth","From Egypt only","From the south country","From the cities of the Philistines"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 31:8",verse:"Behold, I will bring them from the north country, and gather them from the coasts of the earth, and with them the blind and the lame, the woman with child and her that travaileth with child together: a great company shall return thither.", insight:"No distance defeats God's gathering; the north country and the coasts of the earth are all within His reach."},
{q:"Who would return with the great company?", options:["The blind and the lame, the woman with child and her that travaileth with child","Only the strong and the wealthy","Only the priests and the princes","Only the young men fit for war"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 31:8",verse:"Behold, I will bring them from the north country, and gather them from the coasts of the earth, and with them the blind and the lame, the woman with child and her that travaileth with child together: a great company shall return thither.", insight:"The blind, the lame, and the mothers come too; God's rescue is built for the weakest travelers, not the fittest."},
{q:"How would God lead the returning exiles?", options:["With weeping and supplications, by rivers of waters in a straight way","With chariots and armed horsemen","In silence through the desert","By the long road of the sea"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 31:9",verse:"They shall come with weeping, and with supplications will I lead them: I will cause them to walk by the rivers of waters in a straight way, wherein they shall not stumble: for I am a father to Israel, and Ephraim is my firstborn.", insight:"Weeping does not disqualify the journey; God leads the broken gently, on straight paths where they shall not stumble."},
{q:"How does God describe Himself toward Israel?", options:["I am a father to Israel, and Ephraim is my firstborn","I am a master to Israel, and Judah is my servant","I am a judge to Israel, and Ephraim is my burden","I am a stranger to Israel, and Judah is far off"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 31:9",verse:"They shall come with weeping, and with supplications will I lead them: I will cause them to walk by the rivers of waters in a straight way, wherein they shall not stumble: for I am a father to Israel, and Ephraim is my firstborn.", insight:"Father and firstborn; God's relationship with His people is family, not employment, and family means mercy."},
{q:"What does the scattering Shepherd do for Israel?", options:["He that scattered Israel will gather him, and keep him, as a shepherd doth his flock","He leaves them to wander forever","He scatters them among the flocks","He keeps them as hirelings"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 31:10",verse:"Hear the word of the LORD, O ye nations, and declare it in the isles afar off, and say, He that scattered Israel will gather him, and keep him, as a shepherd doth his flock.", insight:"The same hand that scattered will gather; judgment and mercy both belong to the one Shepherd."},
{q:"What would God do with their mourning?", options:["Turn their mourning into joy, and comfort them","Turn their mourning into silence","Let their mourning last a lifetime","Hide their mourning from the nations"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 31:13",verse:"Then shall the virgin rejoice in the dance, both young men and old together: for I will turn their mourning into joy, and will comfort them, and make them rejoice from their sorrow.", insight:"God does not merely end mourning, He turns it; the same heart that wept becomes the one that dances."},
{q:"Whose voice was heard in Ramah?", options:["Rahel weeping for her children, who refused to be comforted","Sarah laughing at the promise","Rebekah blessing her sons","Leah mourning for her husband"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 31:15",verse:"Thus saith the LORD; A voice was heard in Ramah, lamentation, and bitter weeping; Rahel weeping for her children refused to be comforted for her children, because they were not.", insight:"Rachel, the mother of the tribes, weeps for her children; God's heart breaks with every grieving mother."},
{q:"What was Rachel told about her tears?", options:["Refrain thy voice from weeping; thy work shall be rewarded","Weep on, for there is no hope","Forget thy children, for they are gone","Hide thy tears from the enemy"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 31:16",verse:"Thus saith the LORD; Refrain thy voice from weeping, and thine eyes from tears: for thy work shall be rewarded, saith the LORD; and they shall come again from the land of the enemy.", insight:"Even Rachel's tears have wages; God keeps a ledger of every sorrow and pays it back in restoration."},
{q:"What did Ephraim pray after being chastised?", options:["Turn thou me, and I shall be turned","Leave me, for I am lost","Punish me, but never call me","I will turn myself by my own strength"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 31:18",verse:"I have surely heard Ephraim bemoaning himself thus; Thou hast chastised me, and I was chastised, as a bullock unaccustomed to the yoke: turn thou me, and I shall be turned; for thou art the LORD my God.", insight:"True repentance asks God to do the turning; the chastised heart knows it cannot fix itself."},
{q:"How does God feel toward backsliding Ephraim?", options:["My bowels are troubled for him; I will surely have mercy upon him","My anger will never be turned away","I have forgotten him utterly","I will treat him as a stranger"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 31:20",verse:"Is Ephraim my dear son? is he a pleasant child? for since I spake against him, I do earnestly remember him still: therefore my bowels are troubled for him; I will surely have mercy upon him, saith the LORD.", insight:"God's inmost being is troubled with compassion for the runaway; mercy is not reluctant in Him, it is eager."},
{q:"What was Israel told to set up for the return?", options:["Waymarks and high heaps, setting the heart toward the highway","Walls and towers against the enemy","Altars on every high hill","Borders that none may cross"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 31:21",verse:"Set thee up waymarks, make thee high heaps: set thine heart toward the highway, even the way which thou wentest: turn again, O virgin of Israel, turn again to these thy cities.", insight:"Waymarks point the way home before the journey starts; God marks the road of return so no prodigal misses it."},
{q:"What question does God ask the backsliding daughter?", options:["How long wilt thou go about, O thou backsliding daughter?","How long wilt thou stay in the land of thy fathers?","How long wilt thou wait for my promise?","How long wilt thou serve in a strange land?"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 31:22",verse:"How long wilt thou go about, O thou backsliding daughter? for the LORD hath created a new thing in the earth, A woman shall compass a man.", insight:"Wandering is not freedom; God calls the backslider by name and asks how long the detour will last."},
{q:"What proverb would they say no more?", options:["The fathers have eaten a sour grape, and the children's teeth are set on edge","The fathers have planted, and the children reap","The fathers have sinned, and the children are blessed","The fathers have died, and the children live"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 31:29",verse:"In those days they shall say no more, The fathers have eaten a sour grape, and the children’s teeth are set on edge.", insight:"No more blaming the fathers; under the new covenant each soul answers for its own iniquity before God."},
{q:"Whom would the new covenant be made with?", options:["The house of Israel, and the house of Judah","The house of David only","The priests and the Levites","The nations round about"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 31:31",verse:"Behold, the days come, saith the LORD, that I will make a new covenant with the house of Israel, and with the house of Judah:", insight:"God's new covenant reunites the divided kingdom; His grace heals the oldest fractures first."},
{q:"What would God do under the new covenant?", options:["Put His law in their inward parts, and write it in their hearts","Write His law again on tablets of stone","Give them a new set of sacrifices","Remove the law altogether"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 31:33",verse:"But this shall be the covenant that I will make with the house of Israel; After those days, saith the LORD, I will put my law in their inward parts, and write it in their hearts; and will be their God, and they shall be my people.", insight:"Stone tablets could be broken; a law written on the heart cannot be dropped, only loved."},
{q:"What two promises seal the new covenant?", options:["I will forgive their iniquity, and I will remember their sin no more","I will bless their fields, and multiply their flocks","I will give them victory, and make them kings","I will lengthen their days, and enrich their houses"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 31:34",verse:"And they shall teach no more every man his neighbour, and every man his brother, saying, Know the LORD: for they shall all know me, from the least of them unto the greatest of them, saith the LORD; for I will forgive their iniquity, and I will remember their sin no more.", insight:"Forgiveness plus divine forgetfulness; God does not merely pardon sin, He refuses to keep its record."},
{q:"What heavenly lights does God name as His witnesses?", options:["The sun for a light by day, and the ordinances of the moon and of the stars for a light by night","The clouds of heaven and the winds","The rainbow and the morning dew","The lightning and the thunder"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 31:35",verse:"Thus saith the LORD, which giveth the sun for a light by day, and the ordinances of the moon and of the stars for a light by night, which divideth the sea when the waves thereof roar; The LORD of hosts is his name:", insight:"God stakes His promise on the sunrise; as long as the sun rises, Israel's covenant stands."},
]},
{n:32, name:"The Field at Anathoth", seal:{ref:"Jeremiah 32:17",verse:"Ah Lord GOD! behold, thou hast made the heaven and the earth by thy great power and stretched out arm, and there is nothing too hard for thee:"}, facts:["Did you know Jeremiah bought a field while Jerusalem was under siege, paying seventeen shekels of silver for land the enemy was about to overrun? The purchase was a signed, witnessed prophecy that normal life would return (Jeremiah 32:6-15).","Did you know the sealed deed was stored in an earthen jar 'that they may continue many days'? Clay jars preserved documents for centuries, and such finds still turn up in archaeology today (Jeremiah 32:14).","Did you know Jeremiah's prayer, 'there is nothing too hard for thee,' is answered by God with the same words turned back as a question: 'is there any thing too hard for me?' (Jeremiah 32:17, 27)."], questions:[
{q:"In whose reign did the word come to Jeremiah?", options:["The tenth year of Zedekiah king of Judah, the eighteenth year of Nebuchadrezzar","The first year of Jehoiakim","The twentieth year of Josiah","The fifth year of Jehoiachin"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 32:1",verse:"The word that came to Jeremiah from the LORD in the tenth year of Zedekiah king of Judah, which was the eighteenth year of Nebuchadrezzar.", insight:"God dates His word precisely; prophecy is history told in advance, not vague feeling."},
{q:"Where was Jeremiah when he bought the field?", options:["Shut up in the court of the prison, in the king of Judah's house","Free in the temple courts","Hiding in the caves of Adullam","Exiled in Babylon"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 32:2",verse:"For then the king of Babylon’s army besieged Jerusalem: and Jeremiah the prophet was shut up in the court of the prison, which was in the king of Judah’s house.", insight:"God's strangest commands often come in the darkest confinements; a prison court became a property office."},
{q:"Who came to Jeremiah offering a field?", options:["Hanameel the son of Shallum, his uncle","Baruch the son of Neriah","Gedaliah the son of Ahikam","Ebedmelech the Ethiopian"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 32:7",verse:"Behold, Hanameel the son of Shallum thine uncle shall come unto thee, saying, Buy thee my field that is in Anathoth: for the right of redemption is thine to buy it.", insight:"The offer came exactly as God foretold; when God predicts the details, obedience becomes simple recognition."},
{q:"How much silver did Jeremiah weigh out for the field?", options:["Seventeen shekels of silver","Seventy shekels of silver","Seven talents of gold","Thirty pieces of silver"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 32:9",verse:"And I bought the field of Hanameel my uncle’s son, that was in Anathoth, and weighed him the money, even seventeen shekels of silver.", insight:"Seventeen shekels for a field inside a doomed city; faith sometimes pays full price for what only God can see."},
{q:"What did Jeremiah do to make the purchase legal?", options:["Subscribed the evidence, sealed it, took witnesses, and weighed the money in the balances","Shook hands before the elders","Swore an oath by the temple","Paid in secret with no record"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 32:10",verse:"And I subscribed the evidence, and sealed it, and took witnesses, and weighed him the money in the balances.", insight:"Prophecy can be filed in triplicate; God's promises are worth proper paperwork and honest witnesses."},
{q:"To whom did Jeremiah give the deed of purchase?", options:["Baruch the son of Neriah, the son of Maaseiah","Hanameel his uncle's son","Zedekiah the king","The priests of Anathoth"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 32:12",verse:"And I gave the evidence of the purchase unto Baruch the son of Neriah, the son of Maaseiah, in the sight of Hanameel mine uncle’s son, and in the presence of the witnesses that subscribed the book of the purchase, before all the Jews that sat in the court of the prison.", insight:"Baruch, the faithful scribe, carried the deed; God gives His servants trustworthy hands for His documents."},
{q:"Where were the deeds to be stored?", options:["In an earthen vessel, that they may continue many days","In the king's treasury","Under the altar of the temple","In a chest of cedar"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 32:14",verse:"Thus saith the LORD of hosts, the God of Israel; Take these evidences, this evidence of the purchase, both which is sealed, and this evidence which is open; and put them in an earthen vessel, that they may continue many days.", insight:"Clay outlasts kingdoms; God preserves His promises in humble containers that survive the fire."},
{q:"What did the purchase prophesy?", options:["Houses and fields and vineyards shall be possessed again in this land","The land shall lie desolate forever","The people shall never return","The fields shall belong to Babylon"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 32:15",verse:"For thus saith the LORD of hosts, the God of Israel; Houses and fields and vineyards shall be possessed again in this land.", insight:"A deed is a down payment on the future; buying land in a besieged city preached that exile would end."},
{q:"What did Jeremiah confess about God's power?", options:["Thou hast made the heaven and the earth by thy great power, and there is nothing too hard for thee","Thy power is limited by the armies of Babylon","Thou hast made only the earth, and heaven is another's","Thy power was great in Egypt, but is spent"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 32:17",verse:"Ah Lord GOD! behold, thou hast made the heaven and the earth by thy great power and stretched out arm, and there is nothing too hard for thee:", insight:"Creation is the proof of capability; the God who stretched out the heavens can handle a siege."},
{q:"What are God's eyes open upon?", options:["All the ways of the sons of men, to give every one according to his ways","Only the temple and its courts","The armies of the nations","The stars of heaven"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 32:19",verse:"Great in counsel, and mighty in work: for thine eyes are open upon all the ways of the sons of men: to give every one according to his ways, and according to the fruit of his doings:", insight:"Nothing is unobserved; God's open eyes mean both that sin is seen and that suffering is noticed."},
{q:"With what did God bring Israel out of Egypt?", options:["With signs, and with wonders, and with a strong hand, and with a stretched out arm, and with great terror","With quiet persuasion and kind words","With the armies of Israel","With gold and silver alone"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 32:21",verse:"And hast brought forth thy people Israel out of the land of Egypt with signs, and with wonders, and with a strong hand, and with a stretched out arm, and with great terror;", insight:"Jeremiah preaches God's past power to argue His future mercy; remember the Exodus when the siege looks final."},
{q:"Why had all this evil come upon them?", options:["They obeyed not His voice, neither walked in His law","They were too few in number","The nations were stronger than God","They lacked weapons for war"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 32:23",verse:"And they came in, and possessed it; but they obeyed not thy voice, neither walked in thy law; they have done nothing of all that thou commandedst them to do: therefore thou hast caused all this evil to come upon them:", insight:"Jeremiah names the cause plainly; honesty about sin is the first step back to the God who forgives it."},
{q:"How did Jeremiah answer God's command to buy?", options:["Buy thee the field for money, and take witnesses, for the city is given into the hand of the Chaldeans","I will buy, for the city is safe","I refuse, for the price is too high","I will buy, but only in secret"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 32:25",verse:"And thou hast said unto me, O Lord GOD, Buy thee the field for money, and take witnesses; for the city is given into the hand of the Chaldeans.", insight:"Jeremiah's honest question is not rebellion; bringing our confusion to God is itself an act of trust."},
{q:"How did God answer Jeremiah's prayer?", options:["Behold, I am the LORD, the God of all flesh: is there any thing too hard for me?","Thy prayer is too late","I cannot help this time","Ask the king for help"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 32:27",verse:"Behold, I am the LORD, the God of all flesh: is there any thing too hard for me?", insight:"God answers 'nothing is too hard' with a question; the God of all flesh invites us to reason from who He is."},
{q:"What would the Chaldeans do to the city?", options:["Set fire on it, and burn it with the houses","Rebuild its walls","Make it their new capital","Leave it untouched"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 32:29",verse:"And the Chaldeans, that fight against this city, shall come and set fire on this city, and burn it with the houses, upon whose roofs they have offered incense unto Baal, and poured out drink offerings unto other gods, to provoke me to anger.", insight:"Judgment was certain, yet God still planned restoration; the fire does not get the last word."},
{q:"What abomination had they committed in the valley of Hinnom?", options:["Built high places of Baal, to cause their sons and daughters to pass through the fire unto Molech","Built altars to the LORD of hosts","Offered the firstfruits of the harvest","Set up pillars of remembrance"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 32:35",verse:"And they built the high places of Baal, which are in the valley of the son of Hinnom, to cause their sons and their daughters to pass through the fire unto Molech; which I commanded them not, neither came it into my mind, that they should do this abomination, to cause Judah to sin.", insight:"Child sacrifice to Molech was the depth of Judah's fall; God names the horror because He will not excuse it."},
{q:"What would God do with His scattered people?", options:["Gather them out of all countries, and bring them again unto this place, to dwell safely","Scatter them further forever","Leave them among the nations","Gather only the righteous few"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 32:37",verse:"Behold, I will gather them out of all countries, whither I have driven them in mine anger, and in my fury, and in great wrath; and I will bring them again unto this place, and I will cause them to dwell safely:", insight:"Gathering follows scattering in God's grammar; exile is a comma, not a period, in His sentence over His people."},
{q:"What new heart would God give them?", options:["One heart, and one way, that they may fear Him for ever","Many hearts, for many gods","A heart of stone, that they feel nothing","A divided heart, for wisdom"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 32:39",verse:"And I will give them one heart, and one way, that they may fear me for ever, for the good of them, and of their children after them:", insight:"One heart and one way; God's cure for a divided people is a unified devotion that outlives them into their children."},
{q:"What kind of covenant would God make?", options:["An everlasting covenant, that He will not turn away from them, to do them good","A covenant for seventy years only","A covenant of works and wages","A covenant with the priests alone"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 32:40",verse:"And I will make an everlasting covenant with them, that I will not turn away from them, to do them good; but I will put my fear in their hearts, that they shall not depart from me.", insight:"Everlasting, and one-sided in its mercy; God binds Himself to keep doing good to people who kept doing evil."},
{q:"What would men do again in the desolate land?", options:["Buy fields for money, subscribe evidences, seal them, and take witnesses","Flee the land forever","Burn the deeds of purchase","Divide the land by lots of war"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 32:44",verse:"Men shall buy fields for money, and subscribe evidences, and seal them, and take witnesses in the land of Benjamin, and in the places about Jerusalem, and in the cities of Judah, and in the cities of the mountains, and in the cities of the valley, and in the cities of the south: for I will cause their captivity to return, saith the LORD.", insight:"The chapter ends where it began, with a deed; God's last word over desolation is a real estate transaction."}
]}
,
{n:33, name:"Call Unto Me", seal:{ref:"Jeremiah 33:3",verse:"Call unto me, and I will answer thee, and shew thee great and mighty things, which thou knowest not."}, facts:["Did you know 'Call unto me, and I will answer thee' (Jeremiah 33:3) is one of the Bible's great prayer promises, spoken to a prophet sitting in prison while his city was under siege?","Did you know 'the Branch of righteousness' (Jeremiah 33:15) is a title for the promised Messiah, sprouting from David's royal line? Christians read its fulfillment in Jesus.","Did you know God backs His promises with His covenant of day and night (Jeremiah 33:20)? As surely as morning follows night, His word to David stands."], questions:[
{q:"When did the word of the LORD come to Jeremiah the second time?", options:["While he was yet shut up in the court of the prison","After he was set free from prison","While he hid in the wilderness","After Jerusalem had fallen"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 33:1",verse:"Moreover the word of the LORD came unto Jeremiah the second time, while he was yet shut up in the court of the prison, saying,", insight:"God's word finds His servants in prison; stone walls cannot shut out revelation."},
{q:"How is the LORD described at the opening of this word?", options:["The maker thereof, the LORD that formed it, to establish it","The silent watcher of the nations","The God of the distant hills","The destroyer of the city"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 33:2",verse:"Thus saith the LORD the maker thereof, the LORD that formed it, to establish it; the LORD is his name;", insight:"Maker, former, establisher; the God who speaks is the God who made, and what He makes He upholds."},
{q:"What does God promise those who call unto Him?", options:["I will answer thee, and shew thee great and mighty things, which thou knowest not","I will give thee wealth and honor","I will silence thy enemies at once","I will make thee a king over nations"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 33:3",verse:"Call unto me, and I will answer thee, and shew thee great and mighty things, which thou knowest not.", insight:"Call, and God answers with revelation; prayer is the doorway to things too great for us to know alone."},
{q:"What would the people's fighting against the Chaldeans accomplish?", options:["Only fill the houses with the dead bodies of men slain in His anger","Drive the Chaldeans from the land","Win back the cities of Judah","Turn the king's heart to mercy"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 33:5",verse:"They come to fight with the Chaldeans, but it is to fill them with the dead bodies of men, whom I have slain in mine anger and in my fury, and for all whose wickedness I have hid my face from this city.", insight:"Fighting God-ordained judgment only multiplies graves; some battles are lost the moment we refuse to repent."},
{q:"What would God bring to the wounded city?", options:["Health and cure, and the abundance of peace and truth","A new wall of brass","An army of angels","Gold from the nations"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 33:6",verse:"Behold, I will bring it health and cure, and I will cure them, and will reveal unto them the abundance of peace and truth.", insight:"Health, cure, peace, truth; God's restoration heals before it defends, mending the wound before rebuilding the wall."},
{q:"Whose captivity would God cause to return?", options:["The captivity of Judah and the captivity of Israel","The captivity of Egypt","The captivity of Babylon","The captivity of the Philistines"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 33:7",verse:"And I will cause the captivity of Judah and the captivity of Israel to return, and will build them, as at the first.", insight:"Judah and Israel together; God's gathering reunites what sin divided, building them as at the first."},
{q:"What would God do with all their iniquity?", options:["Cleanse them from it, and pardon all their iniquities","Remember it forever","Punish it sevenfold","Hide it from the nations"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 33:8",verse:"And I will cleanse them from all their iniquity, whereby they have sinned against me; and I will pardon all their iniquities, whereby they have sinned, and whereby they have transgressed against me.", insight:"Cleanse and pardon; God does not whitewash sin, He washes it away entirely."},
{q:"What would Jerusalem become to God?", options:["A name of joy, a praise and an honour before all the nations","A name of fear among the nations","A forgotten ruin","A byword of shame"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 33:9",verse:"And it shall be to me a name of joy, a praise and an honour before all the nations of the earth, which shall hear all the good that I do unto them: and they shall fear and tremble for all the goodness and for all the prosperity that I procure unto it.", insight:"A name of joy; the city that shamed God would one day make Him praised among the nations."},
{q:"Where would joy be heard again?", options:["In this place, which ye say shall be desolate without man and without beast","Only in the temple courts","In the palaces of Babylon","In the wilderness of Moab"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 33:10",verse:"Thus saith the LORD; Again there shall be heard in this place, which ye say shall be desolate without man and without beast, even in the cities of Judah, and in the streets of Jerusalem, that are desolate, without man, and without inhabitant, and without beast,", insight:"Joy returns to the very streets called desolate; God loves to contradict our verdicts of hopelessness."},
{q:"Which voices would sound in the streets of Jerusalem?", options:["The voice of joy and gladness, the voice of the bridegroom and the voice of the bride","The voice of weeping and wailing","The voice of merchants and traders","The voice of soldiers on the wall"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 33:11",verse:"The voice of joy, and the voice of gladness, the voice of the bridegroom, and the voice of the bride, the voice of them that shall say, Praise the LORD of hosts: for the LORD is good; for his mercy endureth for ever: and of them that shall bring the sacrifice of praise into the house of the LORD. For I will cause to return the captivity of the land, as at the first, saith the LORD.", insight:"Wedding songs in streets of siege; God's restoration sounds like a marriage, not a funeral."},
{q:"What would the returning worshippers say?", options:["Praise the LORD of hosts: for the LORD is good; for his mercy endureth for ever","Woe unto us, for we are undone","Blessed be the king of Babylon","The LORD has forgotten us"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 33:11",verse:"The voice of joy, and the voice of gladness, the voice of the bridegroom, and the voice of the bride, the voice of them that shall say, Praise the LORD of hosts: for the LORD is good; for his mercy endureth for ever: and of them that shall bring the sacrifice of praise into the house of the LORD. For I will cause to return the captivity of the land, as at the first, saith the LORD.", insight:"His mercy endureth for ever; the oldest chorus in Israel becomes the anthem of the return."},
{q:"What would the desolate land become?", options:["An habitation of shepherds causing their flocks to lie down","A den of wild beasts","A field of battle","A desert of salt"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 33:12",verse:"Thus saith the LORD of hosts; Again in this place, which is desolate without man and without beast, and in all the cities thereof, shall be an habitation of shepherds causing their flocks to lie down.", insight:"Shepherds where soldiers marched; peace turns battlegrounds into pastures."},
{q:"What good thing would God perform?", options:["That good thing which He had promised unto the house of Israel and to the house of Judah","A new temple of gold","A covenant with Babylon","The destruction of the nations"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 33:14",verse:"Behold, the days come, saith the LORD, that I will perform that good thing which I have promised unto the house of Israel and to the house of Judah.", insight:"God performs what He promised; His word is not a wish but a schedule."},
{q:"What would God cause to grow up unto David?", options:["The Branch of righteousness","A cedar of Lebanon","A vine of judgment","A thorn of affliction"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 33:15",verse:"In those days, and at that time, will I cause the Branch of righteousness to grow up unto David; and he shall execute judgment and righteousness in the land.", insight:"The Branch of righteousness; from David's cut-down stump God grows the promised King."},
{q:"What would the Branch execute in the land?", options:["Judgment and righteousness","War and vengeance","Taxes and tribute","Silence and fear"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 33:15",verse:"In those days, and at that time, will I cause the Branch of righteousness to grow up unto David; and he shall execute judgment and righteousness in the land.", insight:"Judgment and righteousness together; the coming King rights wrongs and makes things right."},
{q:"By what name would Jerusalem be called?", options:["The LORD our righteousness","The city of David forever","The queen of nations","The holy mountain"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 33:16",verse:"In those days shall Judah be saved, and Jerusalem shall dwell safely: and this is the name wherewith she shall be called, The LORD our righteousness.", insight:"The city takes God's own name as her title; His righteousness becomes her identity."},
{q:"What promise did God make concerning David's throne?", options:["David shall never want a man to sit upon the throne of the house of Israel","David's line shall end with Zedekiah","The throne shall pass to Babylon","No king shall ever rule again"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 33:17",verse:"For thus saith the LORD; David shall never want a man to sit upon the throne of the house of Israel;", insight:"Never want a man; God's promise to David outlives every fallen king on the throne."},
{q:"What promise did God make concerning the Levites?", options:["They shall never want a man to offer burnt offerings and do sacrifice continually","Their priesthood shall cease forever","They shall serve only in Babylon","Their sacrifices shall be forgotten"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 33:18",verse:"Neither shall the priests the Levites want a man before me to offer burnt offerings, and to kindle meat offerings, and to do sacrifice continually.", insight:"A perpetual priesthood; worship, like the kingdom, rests on God's oath, not man's performance."},
{q:"What unbreakable covenant guarantees these promises?", options:["His covenant of the day and of the night, that day and night come in their season","His covenant with the sea","His covenant with the mountains","His covenant with the stars alone"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 33:20",verse:"Thus saith the LORD; If ye can break my covenant of the day, and my covenant of the night, and that there should not be day and night in their season;", insight:"Break day and night if you can; God's promises are riveted to the turning of the earth."},
{q:"Why would God not cast away the seed of Jacob?", options:["For He would cause their captivity to return, and have mercy on them","For they had earned His favor","For the nations demanded it","For the temple could not be rebuilt"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 33:26",verse:"Then will I cast away the seed of Jacob, and David my servant, so that I will not take any of his seed to be rulers over the seed of Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob: for I will cause their captivity to return, and have mercy on them.", insight:"Mercy, not merit, is the reason; God's decision to restore rests on His compassion, not their record."}
]},
{n:34, name:"Liberty Broken", seal:{ref:"Jeremiah 34:17",verse:"Therefore thus saith the LORD; Ye have not hearkened unto me, in proclaiming liberty, every one to his brother, and every man to his neighbour: behold, I proclaim a liberty for you, saith the LORD, to the sword, to the pestilence, and to the famine; and I will make you to be removed into all the kingdoms of the earth."}, facts:["Did you know the people freed their Hebrew slaves during the siege, then dragged them back into bondage when the danger eased? God called it polluting His name (Jeremiah 34:8-16).","Did you know cutting a calf in two and walking between the pieces was a solemn oath ritual? It meant, 'may what happened to this animal happen to me if I break my word' (Jeremiah 34:18).","Did you know Zedekiah was promised he would 'die in peace,' yet he saw his sons slain, was blinded, and was carried to Babylon? The promise spared him the sword, not the sorrow (Jeremiah 34:4-5)."], questions:[
{q:"When did this word come to Jeremiah?", options:["When Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon and all his army fought against Jerusalem","When the city had already fallen","When Zedekiah made peace with Babylon","When Jeremiah was in Egypt"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 34:1",verse:"The word which came unto Jeremiah from the LORD, when Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon, and all his army, and all the kingdoms of the earth of his dominion, and all the people, fought against Jerusalem, and against all the cities thereof, saying,", insight:"God speaks plainly in the middle of the crisis; prophecy is clearest when the siege ramps are at the wall."},
{q:"What would the king of Babylon do to the city?", options:["Take it, and burn it with fire","Rebuild its walls","Make it his new capital","Leave it untouched"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 34:2",verse:"Thus saith the LORD, the God of Israel; Go and speak to Zedekiah king of Judah, and tell him, Thus saith the LORD; Behold, I will give this city into the hand of the king of Babylon, and he shall burn it with fire:", insight:"Fire was the sentence; God had warned for years, and the warning was about to become history."},
{q:"What would happen to Zedekiah himself?", options:["He would be taken, see the king of Babylon eye to eye, and go to Babylon","He would escape to Egypt","He would die on the battlefield","He would be crowned again in Jerusalem"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 34:3",verse:"And thou shalt not escape out of his hand, but shalt surely be taken, and delivered into his hand; and thine eyes shall behold the eyes of the king of Babylon, and he shall speak with thee mouth to mouth, and thou shalt go to Babylon.", insight:"Eye to eye with the conqueror; running from God's word always ends in facing what we feared."},
{q:"What mercy was promised to Zedekiah?", options:["Thou shalt not die by the sword","Thou shalt escape to Egypt","Thou shalt keep thy throne","Thou shalt see thy sons reign"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 34:4",verse:"Yet hear the word of the LORD, O Zedekiah king of Judah; Thus saith the LORD of thee, Thou shalt not die by the sword:", insight:"Not by the sword; even in judgment God measures mercy, sparing the king the death he dreaded most."},
{q:"How would Zedekiah be honored at his death?", options:["He would die in peace, with odours burned for him, and lamented, saying, Ah lord","He would be buried as a traitor","He would die alone in a dungeon","He would be burned with the city"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 34:5",verse:"But thou shalt die in peace: and with the burnings of thy fathers, the former kings which were before thee, so shall they burn odours for thee; and they will lament thee, saying, Ah lord! for I have pronounced the word, saith the LORD.", insight:"A king's funeral for a failed king; God's mercy gives honor where the record earned none."},
{q:"Where did Jeremiah speak all these words to Zedekiah?", options:["In Jerusalem","In Babylon","In the wilderness","In Egypt"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 34:6",verse:"Then Jeremiah the prophet spake all these words unto Zedekiah king of Judah in Jerusalem,", insight:"Jeremiah spoke to the king's face in the capital; true prophets do not whisper from a safe distance."},
{q:"Which defenced cities still remained in Judah?", options:["Lachish and Azekah","Hebron and Beersheba","Gibeon and Mizpah","Ashkelon and Gaza"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 34:7",verse:"When the king of Babylon’s army fought against Jerusalem, and against all the cities of Judah that were left, against Lachish, and against Azekah: for these defenced cities remained of the cities of Judah.", insight:"Two fortress cities held out while the rest fell; God's record keeps the details that history books confirm."},
{q:"What covenant did Zedekiah make with all the people?", options:["To proclaim liberty unto them","To pay tribute to Babylon","To rebuild the temple","To crown a new king"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 34:8",verse:"This is the word that came unto Jeremiah from the LORD, after that the king Zedekiah had made a covenant with all the people which were at Jerusalem, to proclaim liberty unto them;", insight:"Liberty proclaimed in a besieged city; even desperate kings sometimes stumble onto God's own law."},
{q:"Who was to be set free?", options:["Every Hebrew manservant and maidservant, that none should serve himself of a Jew his brother","Only the servants of the princes","Only those who could pay","Only the foreigners in the land"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 34:9",verse:"That every man should let his manservant, and every man his maidservant, being an Hebrew or an Hebrewess, go free; that none should serve himself of them, to wit, of a Jew his brother.", insight:"No Jew enslaving a Jew; God's law protected His people from devouring one another."},
{q:"How did the people first respond to the covenant?", options:["They obeyed, and let them go","They refused outright","They delayed for a year","They freed only half"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 34:10",verse:"Now when all the princes, and all the people, which had entered into the covenant, heard that every one should let his manservant, and every one his maidservant, go free, that none should serve themselves of them any more, then they obeyed, and let them go.", insight:"They obeyed, and let them go; a right act done for wrong reasons still counts as obedience, until it is undone."},
{q:"What did they do afterward?", options:["They turned, and caused the freed servants to return into subjection","They freed the rest of the slaves","They confirmed the covenant forever","They sent the servants to Babylon"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 34:11",verse:"But afterward they turned, and caused the servants and the handmaids, whom they had let go free, to return, and brought them into subjection for servants and for handmaids.", insight:"Freedom revoked when the pressure eased; repentance that lasts only through the crisis is not repentance."},
{q:"When had God first made this covenant of release?", options:["In the day He brought their fathers out of Egypt, out of the house of bondmen","At Mount Sinai with Moses alone","In the days of David","During the reign of Josiah"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 34:13",verse:"Thus saith the LORD, the God of Israel; I made a covenant with your fathers in the day that I brought them forth out of the land of Egypt, out of the house of bondmen, saying,", insight:"A people freed from bondage must not become bondmasters; redemption received demands liberation given."},
{q:"What was the law of release?", options:["At the end of seven years, after six years of service, let the Hebrew servant go free","After fifty years, let all debts be paid","Never release a servant","After three years, sell him again"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 34:14",verse:"At the end of seven years let ye go every man his brother an Hebrew, which hath been sold unto thee; and when he hath served thee six years, thou shalt let him go free from thee: but your fathers hearkened not unto me, neither inclined their ear.", insight:"Six years of service, then freedom; God's economy never made bondage permanent among His people."},
{q:"What had the people done right at first?", options:["Proclaimed liberty every man to his neighbour, in a covenant made before God in His house","Offered sacrifices for the king","Built new walls for the city","Paid the tribute to Babylon"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 34:15",verse:"And ye were now turned, and had done right in my sight, in proclaiming liberty every man to his neighbour; and ye had made a covenant before me in the house which is called by my name:", insight:"They did right in His sight, and in His house; public obedience in God's presence is a serious vow."},
{q:"What did their reversal do to God's name?", options:["They turned and polluted it","They honored it","They hid it from the nations","They praised it in song"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 34:16",verse:"But ye turned and polluted my name, and caused every man his servant, and every man his handmaid, whom ye had set at liberty at their pleasure, to return, and brought them into subjection, to be unto you for servants and for handmaids.", insight:"Broken vows pollute the Name attached to them; God takes personally the promises made in His presence."},
{q:"What liberty did God proclaim in return?", options:["A liberty to the sword, to the pestilence, and to the famine","A liberty to return to Egypt","A liberty to serve Babylon in peace","A liberty from all tribute"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 34:17",verse:"Therefore thus saith the LORD; Ye have not hearkened unto me, in proclaiming liberty, every one to his brother, and every man to his neighbour: behold, I proclaim a liberty for you, saith the LORD, to the sword, to the pestilence, and to the famine; and I will make you to be removed into all the kingdoms of the earth.", insight:"You refused liberty to your brothers, so God proclaims liberty to your enemies; withheld mercy becomes invited judgment."},
{q:"How had they sealed their covenant?", options:["They cut the calf in twain, and passed between the parts thereof","They shook hands before the elders","They signed with the king's seal","They swore by the temple"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 34:18",verse:"And I will give the men that have transgressed my covenant, which have not performed the words of the covenant which they had made before me, when they cut the calf in twain, and passed between the parts thereof,", insight:"They walked between the pieces, calling down the calf's fate on themselves; solemn oaths have solemn consequences."},
{q:"Who had passed between the parts of the calf?", options:["The princes of Judah and Jerusalem, the eunuchs, the priests, and all the people","Only the king and the prophet","Only the priests","Only the elders of the city"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 34:19",verse:"The princes of Judah, and the princes of Jerusalem, the eunuchs, and the priests, and all the people of the land, which passed between the parts of the calf;", insight:"Princes, priests, and people all walked the oath path; when leaders break covenant, the whole nation bears it."},
{q:"What would become of the covenant breakers?", options:["Their dead bodies would be for meat unto the fowls of the heaven and the beasts of the earth","They would be exiled in honor","They would be forgiven at once","They would be made princes in Babylon"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 34:20",verse:"I will even give them into the hand of their enemies, and into the hand of them that seek their life: and their dead bodies shall be for meat unto the fowls of the heaven, and to the beasts of the earth.", insight:"The fate they invoked on themselves; God lets oath-breakers meet the very curse they called down."},
{q:"What would God command concerning the city?", options:["Cause the Chaldeans to return to this city, take it, burn it, and make Judah's cities a desolation","Cause Babylon to make peace forever","Cause the siege to be lifted","Cause the city to stand unharmed"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 34:22",verse:"Behold, I will command, saith the LORD, and cause them to return to this city; and they shall fight against it, and take it, and burn it with fire: and I will make the cities of Judah a desolation without an inhabitant.", insight:"He will command, and they will return; even enemy armies march on God's orders."}
]},
{n:35, name:"The Rechabites", seal:{ref:"Jeremiah 35:19",verse:"Therefore thus saith the LORD of hosts, the God of Israel; Jonadab the son of Rechab shall not want a man to stand before me for ever."}, facts:["Did you know Jonadab son of Rechab lived about 250 years before this chapter? He rode with Jehu to purge Baal worship from Israel (2 Kings 10:15-16), and his descendants still kept his rules.","Did you know the Rechabites kept the commands of a dead ancestor while Judah ignored the living God? Their obedience became God's courtroom exhibit against His own people (Jeremiah 35:14-16).","Did you know God's reward to the Rechabites was a lineage, not land? 'Jonadab the son of Rechab shall not want a man to stand before me for ever' (Jeremiah 35:19)."], questions:[
{q:"In whose days did this word come to Jeremiah?", options:["In the days of Jehoiakim the son of Josiah king of Judah","In the days of Zedekiah","In the days of Josiah","In the days of Jehoiachin"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 35:1",verse:"The word which came unto Jeremiah from the LORD in the days of Jehoiakim the son of Josiah king of Judah, saying,", insight:"This lesson predates the siege; God was already gathering His evidence while Judah still had time."},
{q:"What was Jeremiah told to do with the house of the Rechabites?", options:["Bring them into the house of the LORD, into one of the chambers, and give them wine to drink","Drive them out of Jerusalem","Send them to Babylon","Number them for the army"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 35:2",verse:"Go unto the house of the Rechabites, and speak unto them, and bring them into the house of the LORD, into one of the chambers, and give them wine to drink.", insight:"A strange errand with a holy purpose; God staged a test in His own house to teach His own people."},
{q:"Whom did Jeremiah gather?", options:["Jaazaniah the son of Jeremiah, the son of Habaziniah, with his brethren, his sons, and the whole house of the Rechabites","Only the elders of the city","The priests of the temple","The sons of the king"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 35:3",verse:"Then I took Jaazaniah the son of Jeremiah, the son of Habaziniah, and his brethren, and all his sons, and the whole house of the Rechabites;", insight:"The whole house came; obedience was a family affair among the Rechabites, from the eldest to the youngest."},
{q:"Into whose chamber were they brought?", options:["The chamber of the sons of Hanan, the son of Igdaliah, a man of God","The chamber of the king","The chamber of the treasury","The chamber of the guard"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 35:4",verse:"And I brought them into the house of the LORD, into the chamber of the sons of Hanan, the son of Igdaliah, a man of God, which was by the chamber of the princes, which was above the chamber of Maaseiah the son of Shallum, the keeper of the door:", insight:"A man of God's chamber hosted the test; God honors His faithful by giving them front-row seats to His lessons."},
{q:"What did Jeremiah set before them?", options:["Pots full of wine, and cups, saying, Drink ye wine","Bread and water only","Empty cups","Meat from the sacrifices"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 35:5",verse:"And I set before the sons of the house of the Rechabites pots full of wine, and cups, and I said unto them, Drink ye wine.", insight:"Full pots and a direct command; the test was real, public, and impossible to misunderstand."},
{q:"How did the Rechabites answer?", options:["We will drink no wine: for Jonadab the son of Rechab our father commanded us","We will drink, for the prophet commands it","We will drink only a little","We will ask our wives first"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 35:6",verse:"But they said, We will drink no wine: for Jonadab the son of Rechab our father commanded us, saying, Ye shall drink no wine, neither ye, nor your sons for ever:", insight:"They refused a prophet to obey their father; convictions held in God's house are worth more than compliance."},
{q:"How long was Jonadab's command to stand?", options:["For ever: neither ye, nor your sons","For one generation","Until they reached Jerusalem","For seventy years"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 35:6",verse:"But they said, We will drink no wine: for Jonadab the son of Rechab our father commanded us, saying, Ye shall drink no wine, neither ye, nor your sons for ever:", insight:"Forever, and for sons unborn; the Rechabites treated a father's word as a covenant across centuries."},
{q:"What else had Jonadab commanded?", options:["Neither build house, nor sow seed, nor plant vineyard, but dwell in tents all your days","Build a city and name it Rechab","Plant vineyards on every hill","Serve as priests in the temple"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 35:7",verse:"Neither shall ye build house, nor sow seed, nor plant vineyard, nor have any: but all your days ye shall dwell in tents; that ye may live many days in the land where ye be strangers.", insight:"Tents, not houses; Jonadab taught his sons to live as pilgrims, holding this world loosely."},
{q:"Who had kept Jonadab's charge?", options:["We, our wives, our sons, and our daughters, all our days","Only the eldest sons","Only those living in Jerusalem","Only the men of war"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 35:8",verse:"Thus have we obeyed the voice of Jonadab the son of Rechab our father in all that he hath charged us, to drink no wine all our days, we, our wives, our sons, nor our daughters;", insight:"Wives, sons, daughters; the whole family kept the charge, proving obedience can be a household culture."},
{q:"What did the Rechabites own?", options:["No vineyard, nor field, nor seed","Great vineyards and fields","Houses in Jerusalem","Flocks beyond number"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 35:9",verse:"Nor to build houses for us to dwell in: neither have we vineyard, nor field, nor seed:", insight:"No field, no seed, no vineyard; they owned almost nothing, yet they owned their obedience."},
{q:"How had they lived?", options:["They had dwelt in tents, and obeyed all that Jonadab commanded","They had built houses in secret","They had sown seed at night","They had drunk wine in private"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 35:10",verse:"But we have dwelt in tents, and have obeyed, and done according to all that Jonadab our father commanded us.", insight:"Tents and obedience; their lifestyle was their testimony, unchanged for generations."},
{q:"Why were the Rechabites dwelling at Jerusalem?", options:["For fear of the army of the Chaldeans, and for fear of the army of the Syrians","They had been banished there","They came for the feast","They sought the king's favor"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 35:11",verse:"But it came to pass, when Nebuchadrezzar king of Babylon came up into the land, that we said, Come, and let us go to Jerusalem for fear of the army of the Chaldeans, and for fear of the army of the Syrians: so we dwell at Jerusalem.", insight:"War drove them to the city, but it did not drive them from their vows; pressure reveals what promises are made of."},
{q:"What question did God put to the men of Judah?", options:["Will ye not receive instruction to hearken to my words?","Will ye not build me a temple?","Will ye not pay me tribute?","Will ye not flee to Egypt?"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 35:13",verse:"Thus saith the LORD of hosts, the God of Israel; Go and tell the men of Judah and the inhabitants of Jerusalem, Will ye not receive instruction to hearken to my words? saith the LORD.", insight:"Will ye not receive instruction? God's question still stands over every heart that hears and will not heed."},
{q:"What contrast did God draw?", options:["The words of Jonadab are performed, but ye hearkened not unto me, though I spoke rising early","Both Judah and the Rechabites obeyed","Neither obeyed anyone","The Rechabites failed as Judah did"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 35:14",verse:"The words of Jonadab the son of Rechab, that he commanded his sons not to drink wine, are performed; for unto this day they drink none, but obey their father’s commandment: notwithstanding I have spoken unto you, rising early and speaking; but ye hearkened not unto me.", insight:"A dead man's words were kept; the living God's words were not. The contrast is Judah's condemnation."},
{q:"Whom had God sent to Judah?", options:["All His servants the prophets, rising up early and sending them","Only Jeremiah","The kings of Judah","The priests alone"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 35:15",verse:"I have sent also unto you all my servants the prophets, rising up early and sending them, saying, Return ye now every man from his evil way, and amend your doings, and go not after other gods to serve them, and ye shall dwell in the land which I have given to you and to your fathers: but ye have not inclined your ear, nor hearkened unto me.", insight:"Rising early and sending; God's messengers came at dawn, again and again, and were ignored by noon."},
{q:"What had the prophets said?", options:["Return ye now every man from his evil way, and ye shall dwell in the land","Flee to the mountains","Serve the king of Babylon forever","Build walls against the enemy"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 35:15",verse:"I have sent also unto you all my servants the prophets, rising up early and sending them, saying, Return ye now every man from his evil way, and amend your doings, and go not after other gods to serve them, and ye shall dwell in the land which I have given to you and to your fathers: but ye have not inclined your ear, nor hearkened unto me.", insight:"Return, and dwell; every prophet carried the same two words, repentance and its promise."},
{q:"What was Judah's shame beside the Rechabites?", options:["The sons of Jonadab performed their father's commandment, but this people hath not hearkened unto me","Judah obeyed better than they did","Both alike disobeyed","The Rechabites had no commandment"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 35:16",verse:"Because the sons of Jonadab the son of Rechab have performed the commandment of their father, which he commanded them; but this people hath not hearkened unto me:", insight:"Out-obeyed by nomads; when outsiders keep faith better than insiders, the insiders stand judged."},
{q:"What evil would God bring upon Judah?", options:["All the evil He had pronounced, because He spoke and they heard not, called and they answered not","A light affliction for a short time","Nothing, for He had relented","Only a famine of three years"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 35:17",verse:"Therefore thus saith the LORD God of hosts, the God of Israel; Behold, I will bring upon Judah and upon all the inhabitants of Jerusalem all the evil that I have pronounced against them: because I have spoken unto them, but they have not heard; and I have called unto them, but they have not answered.", insight:"Spoken to, but not hearing; called to, but not answering. Judgment is the echo of ignored mercy."},
{q:"Why did Jeremiah bless the Rechabites?", options:["Because they had obeyed the commandment of Jonadab their father, and kept all his precepts","Because they were wealthy","Because they lived in Jerusalem","Because they were priests"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 35:18",verse:"And Jeremiah said unto the house of the Rechabites, Thus saith the LORD of hosts, the God of Israel; Because ye have obeyed the commandment of Jonadab your father, and kept all his precepts, and done according unto all that he hath commanded you:", insight:"God praises borrowed obedience to shame neglected obedience; faithfulness anywhere convicts faithlessness everywhere."},
{q:"What promise was given to Jonadab's line?", options:["Jonadab the son of Rechab shall not want a man to stand before me for ever","They shall inherit the land of Judah","They shall become priests in the temple","They shall rule over Israel"], a:0, ref:"Jeremiah 35:19",verse:"Therefore thus saith the LORD of hosts, the God of Israel; Jonadab the son of Rechab shall not want a man to stand before me for ever.", insight:"A man to stand before Him forever; God rewards quiet, generational faithfulness with an eternal place in His presence."}
]}
]);